# Inspector

- `internal/inspector.Service` owns the installation's Golem Inspector. The hub is a managed container (`docker.HubSpec`, name `golem-deploy_inspector`, label `inspector-hub=true`, found with `Client.InspectorHubs`/`IsInspectorHub` by labels and name, never by a client value). Only the daemon talks to it, over its `127.0.0.1` port with the fixed origin `HubOrigin` and host `hubHost`. Never embed the hub package, never change the engine, never put the hub on a network shared with games (`EnsureNetwork` not isolated fails the apply and removes a hub there).
- The hub port (`hubContainer`/`hubPort`/`hubVerified`/`hubSeq` under `mu`) is valid only for that container while Docker reports it running and publishing that port. Every Docker check of the hub is recorded only through `observeHub` (`startCheck` snapshot, `seenOf`): `Status` (its listing confirms too), `refreshHub` (coalesced through `flight`, `minAge` per caller, skipped inside the flight while a confirmation is fresh), `needsRepair`, `verifyHub`. Three outcomes stay apart: gone or wrong (missing, stopped, not current, no port; `seenGone` also forgets the container) drops the port and makes the repair due; no answer (any error, timeout, cancelled or expired context) drops the port until a later confirmation, state `unknown`, and is never evidence (no repair, no session ended, nothing removed); a confirmation keeps or restores the port with `hubVerified` = when Docker answered. Every change of these fields bumps `hubSeq`; a result of a check that started before a change is discarded. `hubUnreachable` drops the port on any transport error (`transportError`). `hubLogin` runs `verifyHub` (Docker, never cached, own `hubDockerTimeout`) before the password, which goes out on a client without keep-alive (`hubClient.login`). A hub session, the hub agent token and relayed static requests go out only after `freshHub` (Docker confirmation within `hubCheckInterval`, else one coalesced refresh first), asked right before each send, the replay with a renewed hub session included: `retryTransport`, `agentTransport`, `hubTargets` (the hub request's timeout starts after the Docker check), `logoutAt`. `checkHub` (each sweep) asks `/ready`; a result counts only for the container, port and `hubSeq` it started with; `hubSickAfter` failures mark `hubSick`: port dropped, not restored from metadata, `Status` `stopped` with `sickDetail`, repair due, `ensureHub` never keeps that container (nor a kept one that did not become ready: `notReadyError`; a Docker failure in `waitHubReady` fails the job with the Docker message and marks nothing). Requests and relays use one `hubView` snapshot (generation, secrets, container, port) from start to end. `mu` is never held across a Docker or hub call; the network addresses and the target list are refreshed through `flight`.
- Admission: tickets, sessions, agent relays and static page relays (`pages`) are registered only through `admit(gen, ...)` under `mu` while `admitting` is open with the generation the request started with. `endAdmission` (shutdown, listener close), `storeEnabled(false)`, `stopServing` (every failed apply after the image step) and a rotation change `gen` before `drain`, so nothing admitted before survives it. A ticket records its `gen` and is accepted only under it: at consumption, at the hub snapshot and at registration (`admit(tk.gen, ...)`).
- Hub secrets (`hubSecrets`: password and its cost-12 bcrypt hash, hub agent token, per-server token key, generation) are generated on first enable or `rotate_secrets`, sealed with `secrets.InspectorSecretsPurpose` in `settings.inspector_secrets` (durable write) and only reach the hub's env file. Never return, log or put them in argv or labels; config hashes use the generation. Per-server tokens are `gdi_<id>_<hex HMAC-SHA256>` (`agentToken`, constant-time `verifyAgentToken`).
- `inspector.apply` (restricted, subject `inspector/hub`). `Update` answers `ErrUnavailable` (409) with `--inspector-listen off`, validates against the stored setting, writes `settings.inspector_enabled` first and only when `enabled` is in the request (`storeEnabled`; a failed write fails the request; `settingMu` keeps the copy in `enabled` behind the database; off closes admission and drains at once and sets `cleanupDue`), then starts the job. The job reads the setting when it runs (`loadEnabled`): off or unavailable removes everything (`disable`); on runs image (`ResolveImage`; `PullImage` with the build client configuration only when missing or `pull_image`; the automatic repair never pulls) before anything changes, `EnsureNetwork`, secrets (a rotation stores them, bumps `gen`, forgets the old hub and drains), keep a running hub whose `config-hash` label and image match `hubHash` or replace it, `waitHubReady`, daemon login, open the listener, then publish the hub (`hubVerified` is the time of `waitHubReady`'s Docker answer) and open admission in one step under `mu` only while `enabled` is still true (else `errTurnedOff` and the same job runs `cleanup`). Any failure calls `stopServing` (admission closed and drained, hub forgotten, state `stopped`, user-safe `detail`), except an image failure (`unchangedError`) while a hub serves, which keeps it; Docker stderr goes to the daemon log only; hub output goes to the job log through `logsafe` with `hub | ` and secrets redacted. `Run` starts the repair at start when the Inspector is on, or when a hub exists that should not (`hubLeft`; only a completed listing counts as a look); then `sweep` each `SweepInterval`: sessions, agent relays, `refreshHub`, `checkHub`, `needsRepair`, each step with its own `stepTimeout` and nothing once a step's context is done. `needsRepair`: on, at most every `RepairInterval` after a failed attempt (`noteAttempt`); off, a left listener or hub or `cleanupDue` at once and after `cleanupRetry` (`cleanup`), never paced by failed starts, else `hubLeft` every `RepairInterval`. `startRepair` records the attempt (`lastAttempt` or `lastCleanup`) before the job starts, so a job that fails before its body is paced too; `Close` ends everything at shutdown (the listener waits for relays within its grace), the hub keeps running.
- The listener is bound only while the setting is on. With panel TLS it serves the panel certificate to browsers and plaintext to agents on one port (`sniffer`, first byte 0x16, bounded wait; closing it closes waiting sockets); without TLS one plaintext server does both. `limitListener` caps open connections (`maxConns`) for their whole life; `DisableGeneralOptionsHandler` is set; the servers' `ReadTimeout`/`WriteTimeout` (`reqTimeout`) also bound what Go answers before a handler (a 417 and its body drain). Every handler runs under the listener's `tracker`; relayed streams get their client connection through `proxyWriter.Hijack` (deadlines cleared, `trackedConn` closed when the request context ends, CloseWrite hidden), and `proxyWriter` drops the hub's 1xx responses and trailers. `prepare` starts every request: read and write deadlines (`reqTimeout`; a proxied request's write deadline is never after its session's expiry), and refuses announced trailers, GET/HEAD with a body, upgrades other than WebSocket and upgrades with a body; `reject` adds `Connection: close` and a short drain for requests with a body. Never rewrite a request's `Body` or `ContentLength`. Browser requests use the shared `netpolicy.Policy` (Host allowlist plus the `--inspector-public-url` host, `ClientIP`). The agent endpoint skips the Host policy and checks the peer instead (managed network subnets; loopback only off Linux or with `--inspector-agent-host`) and refuses `Forwarded`, `X-Forwarded-For` and `Via`.
- Browser side: no cookie is set or read on the listener (game servers share the host name on other ports); the session key lives in the tab's `sessionStorage` of the Inspector's origin. One-time tickets (32 random bytes, hashed, 60 s, bounded per account and in total) from `CreateSession` travel only in the fragment of `EnterPath` (`#t=`); the target stays bound to the ticket. The daemon's own paths (`ownPrefix`, `browser.go`): the static entry page (CSP `script-src 'self'`, `connect-src 'self'`, style by hash), `enter.js`, `boot.js` (embedded ES5/ASCII files, tested in jsdom by `frontend/src/test/inspector-scripts.test.ts`), the ended page, and `POST SessionPath` (Origin, JSON content type, the failure limiter before the body, 1 KiB; consumes the ticket, checks the account, logs in at the hub, registers the session through `admit`, answers `{key, target_id}` no-store; only the key's hash is kept). Every hub HTML response gets `bootTag` after its leading `<head>` tag (`headEnd`: only a byte order mark, whitespace, one doctype and an `<html>` tag may come first, no comment; `tagEnd` follows the HTML tokenizer, a quote counts only at the start of a value; identity encoding, 2 MiB, Content-Length fixed; anything else, larger, compressed or 206 is a 502 page). `boot.js` stringifies a URL once, resolves it against the document's base URL and passes exactly the resolved URL (or a native copy of a Request) to the native call, for other origins too. A 101 that is not a WebSocket answer to a WebSocket request (`webSocketAnswer`: Upgrade, Connection and one `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` matching the request's key) is `errBadUpgrade` from `ModifyResponse` in both proxies (closes the hub connection). Every relayed upgrade runs under `startHandshake` (`handshakeTimeout`, 30 s) until `ModifyResponse` accepted the 101. `/api`, `/auth`, `/ws` need a session presented as `Authorization: Bearer` or, on upgrades, the subprotocols `WebSocketProtocol` + `WebSocketKeyPrefix<key>` (the 101 then selects the marker); otherwise 401 `auth_required` with `SessionHeader: ended`. Only WebSocket upgrades under `/ws/` are relayed. Other GET/HEAD paths are the static UI, relayed with no credentials and no session lookup. A proxied request checks the key in memory, then the Origin, reads its body, then rechecks the account (exists, approved, same token version) and registers with the session in one step (`session.track`, which refuses an ended or expired session and returns one absolute expiry used for the request's context and its write deadline). `sessionStore` ends a session under its lock before it leaves the store (eviction, expiry, removal, drain) and returns the hub session ids to log out; 8 h lifetime; sign-out (POST without a body) ends the session locally, then logs out at the hub (`logoutAt`). The proxy strips cookies, credentials, subprotocols and forwarding headers, injects only the session's hub cookie, rewrites Origin, drops `Set-Cookie` and `SessionHeader`, never forwards `blockedPaths` (`/auth/login`, `/ws/agent`, `/metrics`, `/health`, `/live`, `/ready`), and requires exactly one Origin, the browser's own in canonical form (`canonicalOrigin`), for WebSockets and non-GET/HEAD. Never log the key, `Authorization` or `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol`. A lost hub session (401, or `/auth/session` false) is renewed once per request through `relogin`; when that fails the request is a 503 (`errHubSessionLost`), an `/auth/session` answer over 4 KiB a 502, and a request whose context ended still gets an answer. Hub logins are serialised (`loginSem`); `loginGuard` holds logins per hub generation (container and secrets' generation) after a 401 (for good) or 429 (until Retry-After), never shortens a hold and resets only when the generation changes. Ended sessions cancel their tracked requests, which closes open WebSocket streams on both sides.
- Agent side: relay `/ws/agent` with the hub's token instead of the server's, one relay per server (a new valid connection closes the previous one) and at most `MaxAgents` in total; `agents.closeAll` on off and rotation; `sweepAgents` closes relays of deleted servers and servers whose switch is off.
- `Agent` implements `servers.Inspector`: endpoint, generation and a token function of that generation in one snapshot, following the desired configuration (not whether the hub runs); after the address lookup it rechecks `enabled` and the same `sec` and takes the port in one step under `mu`. The network address cache (`netAddrs`) is dropped at once when `EnsureNetwork` says not managed or not ours (`dropNetwork`); `netGen` discards the answer of an older query. The servers rule covers injection and the config hash. `GET /api/v1/inspector` (`Status`) reads the hub container and, through a daemon-held hub session, `/api/targets` (cached `targetsTTL`, bounded body, only bounded fields relayed, matched by server name among servers with the switch on).
- Verify with `go test ./internal/inspector/... ./internal/netpolicy/... ./internal/httpapi/...` (unit tests use `testHooks` for the races and a fake hub whose `inspect` stands in for Docker; `lifecycle_test.go` covers turn-off, repair, readiness, network cache and `Agent`; `hubstate_test.go` the three Docker outcomes, the sweep budgets and the repair pacing; `requests_test.go` page relays, eviction, the handshake deadline and the accept check); with Docker, `GOLEM_DEPLOY_DOCKER_TESTS=1 go test -p 1 ./internal/inspector/` builds a stand-in hub image (`inspectortest/standinhub`), applies it for real, checks hardening and labels, serves the static UI with the boot script, exchanges a ticket, proxies a request and a browser WebSocket with the key and relays an agent from a container. Changes to `boot.js` or `enter.js` also need `npm test` in `frontend/`.
